      <span class="p-landmark p-mono"><a href="../index.html">Said Mohaddes Sadeqi</a> · the proof</span>
      <h1>At seventeen, our team of three built a wearable that let a blind person
      hear and feel a room instead of seeing it.</h1>
      <p class="status">
        <span class="p-tag p-mono">Shipped 2022</span>
        <span class="p-mono">Herat, Afghanistan · Samsun, Turkey</span>
      </p>
      <p class="standfirst">Glasses with an ultrasonic sensor and audio feedback,
      shoes that vibrated, and a GPS module. About $112 in total. Field-tested with
      blind users at a support centre in Herat, then taken to TEKNOFEST Black Sea,
      where it won Best Presentation.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="beats" id="beats">

      <section aria-labelledby="b-problem">
        <h2 id="b-problem">The problem</h2>
        <p>In Herat, if you were blind, you got around with a cane or with someone
        else's arm. Both only tell you what is already in front of you. Nothing we
        could find used sound and touch together, to tell a person what was coming
        before the cane found it. We wanted a system that did not put the whole job on
        one sense, because a cane alone means every obstacle is a surprise until the
        last second.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="b-constraint">
        <h2 id="b-constraint">The constraint</h2>
        <p>I was seventeen, team captain of three students at Herat Boys' High School.
        Our team was called ABRT01. We had $112 in total, no lab, and a TEKNOFEST
        deadline that did not move for any of that.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="b-decision">
        <h2 id="b-decision">The decision</h2>
        <p>We built three things instead of one: glasses with an ultrasonic sensor and
        audio feedback, shoes that vibrated, and a GPS module. You got the same
        information through sound and through your feet, rather than from a single
        channel doing all the work.</p>
        <p>That is three separate parts to build, wire, and keep working on $112,
        instead of one polished object, and three parts means more that can fail when
        your whole team is three people. A single device would have been simpler to
        demo and harder to break. We gave that up on purpose. We also brought the
        finished kit to a support centre for the blind in Herat and tested it there
        before the competition, rather than after.</p>
      </section>

